      "writtenOn": "2023-09-28T17:21:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "`mServiceUuids` is shared between encrypted and not encrypted data.\nI see 2 problems with that:\n1. It make it impossible to have both encrypted and not encrypted serviceUuid in the same advertising. I understand that this capability should remain, let me know if I am wrong\n\n2. The last caller of the builder will override the encryption state. It will be errorprone for app developper and they will have to look at the code implementation to fix it\n\n\nMaybe a better API would be (pseudo-code without any check):\n```\npublic Builder addServiceUuid(ParcelUuid serviceUuid) {  \n   mServiceUuids.add(serviceUuid);\n}\npublic Builder addServiceUuidEnc(ParcelUuid serviceUuid) {  \n   mServiceUuidsEnc.add(serviceUuid);\n}\n```\n\nThis comment apply for all other API that take user data",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 490,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 490,
        "endChar": 25
      },
      "revId": "2e40e2cd051c02b2923d5781274eec5413fcf35c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ee207aa3_afcee49f",
        "filename": "framework/java/android/bluetooth/le/AdvertiseData.java",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 490,
      "author": {
        "id": 1443525
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-09-28T19:44:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Spec allows multiple instance of a given AD type but the Android APIs (both Scanner and Advertiser side) are all designed with assumption that a given AD type can occur only once in the Advertising Data. The above implementation is just an extension of that approach. Perhaps something can be added in documentation to clarify this further.",
      "parentUuid": "ee56084e_5793216a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 490,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 490,
        "endChar": 25
      },
      "revId": "2e40e2cd051c02b2923d5781274eec5413fcf35c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "59e78ca3_cb870495",
        "filename": "framework/java/android/bluetooth/le/AdvertiseData.java",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 490,
      "author": {
        "id": 1884477
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-10-02T22:11:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think it is reasonable to make an exception for encrypted data since the API already suggests that two distinct lists (encrypted and not encrypted) are maintained. This is also a feature that users may want to exercise (e.g. hide some service data from untrusted peers). From an implementation point of view it is not adding much work.",
      "parentUuid": "ee207aa3_afcee49f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 490,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 490,
        "endChar": 25
      },
      "revId": "2e40e2cd051c02b2923d5781274eec5413fcf35c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b1195b4a_14b7981d",
        "filename": "framework/java/android/bluetooth/le/AdvertiseData.java",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 490,
      "author": {
        "id": 1127992
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-10-02T23:11:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "+1 - I agree with Henri and William here; it seems reasonable to expect some implementation will want to limit encryption to subset of UUIDs. Similarly we might want the same for service data IMO.\n\nSunny, can you please consider updating the CL to accommodate this?",
      "parentUuid": "59e78ca3_cb870495",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 490,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 490,
        "endChar": 25
      },
      "revId": "2e40e2cd051c02b2923d5781274eec5413fcf35c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1c6b6aef_9e552002",
        "filename": "framework/java/android/bluetooth/le/AdvertiseData.java",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 490,
      "author": {
        "id": 1443525
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-10-02T23:36:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This could be problem if we do scanning and advertising between two identical Android phones. It is not much work for Advertiser side, but on scanner side the stack does the decryption of the encrypted data and appends to the plaintext data and sends it to the framework as byte [] of adv_data. In the framework, this is populated into ScanRecord which cannot distinguish which AD types were encrypted and which were plaintext. There are existing public APIs like ScanRecord::getServiceData( ) which return only one of the AD type structure (either encrypted or plaintext: whatever was populated later in OTA adv data).\nhttps://cs.android.com/android/platform/superproject/main/+/main:packages/modules/Bluetooth/framework/java/android/bluetooth/le/ScanRecord.java;l\u003d389?q\u003dScanRecord.java\u0026ss\u003dandroid%2Fplatform%2Fsuperproject%2Fmain\n\nSo, either we will need to have the above asymmetry on scanner and advertiser side API, OR for scanner, need to populate the \"encrypted\" meta-information from stack to framework and have a corresponding set of \"encrypted\" API in ScanRecord, for eg: ScanRecord::getServiceDataEncrypted( ).\n\nBTW, currently on scanner side there is no changes in framework/SDK API for this feature and the reason for that was to allow existing legacy Apps scan encrypted AD type without updating the App.",
      "parentUuid": "59e78ca3_cb870495",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 490,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 490,
        "endChar": 25
      },
      "revId": "2e40e2cd051c02b2923d5781274eec5413fcf35c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8a86f3ca_a4f2311c",
        "filename": "framework/java/android/bluetooth/le/AdvertiseData.java",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 490,
      "author": {
        "id": 1884477
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-10-02T23:41:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Merging the lists for encrypted and clear text AD types seems like a reasonable compromise, as long as it is properly documented.\n\nThe issue already exist with third party devices and is not limited to android devices: earbuds and other devices may use this feature (i.e. duplicate an AD type in encrypted and clear text), android should gracefully support this and report all available information to the user.",
      "parentUuid": "1c6b6aef_9e552002",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 490,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 490,
        "endChar": 25
      },
      "revId": "2e40e2cd051c02b2923d5781274eec5413fcf35c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "76759ac0_0b9cfd77",
        "filename": "framework/java/android/bluetooth/le/AdvertiseData.java",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 490,
      "author": {
        "id": 1443525
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-10-03T04:15:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Henri, so is the ask to continue to have merging of the decrypted and unencrypted (plaintext) AD types in stack and presented to the framework/App without encryption meta-information? In this case no new API is needed and the existing CL is sufficient. The App will have to rely on ScanRecord::getBytes to get the raw advertisement and parse the two identical AD types since it cannot rely on the existing ScanRecord::get\u003cad_types\u003e",
      "parentUuid": "8a86f3ca_a4f2311c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 490,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 490,
        "endChar": 25
      },
      "revId": "2e40e2cd051c02b2923d5781274eec5413fcf35c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
